1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,110
Have you ever noticed something
strange about vegetables?

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:07,110
Whenever someone says: "You
should eat more vegetables."

3
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:09,510
What do they actually mean?

4
00:00:10,292 --> 00:00:11,372
Tomatoes. Cucumber.

5
00:00:11,452 --> 00:00:14,902
And, in the better case...
a pepper. That's it.

6
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:18,706
Apparently the entire plant
kingdom has been reduced to three

7
00:00:18,786 --> 00:00:23,196
things you can slice into thin
pieces and put next to a schnitzel.

8
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:25,709
And I'm getting a little tired of it.

9
00:00:26,179 --> 00:00:27,789
Especially the tomatoes.

10
00:00:28,831 --> 00:00:32,661
Because unless you have a really good
one, picked when it is actually ripe...

11
00:00:33,631 --> 00:00:38,021
a supermarket tomato can taste like
someone described a tomato to water.

12
00:00:38,770 --> 00:00:43,690
And the cucumber isn't much better.
Crunchy? Yes. Refreshing? Sure.

13
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:47,780
Does it actually taste of
anything? Well... sometimes.

14
00:00:48,801 --> 00:00:52,491
So why do we eat these things all
the time? And more importantly...

15
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:55,451
is there actually any other vegetable?

16
00:00:56,201 --> 00:00:57,911
Spoiler: there are quite a few.

17
00:01:00,251 --> 00:01:01,781
But before we get to those...

18
00:01:01,951 --> 00:01:03,781
that thing about the tomato.

19
00:01:03,951 --> 00:01:05,381
It is not just a feeling.

20
00:01:06,011 --> 00:01:07,921
Somebody went and measured it.

21
00:01:08,571 --> 00:01:11,591
Researchers in Florida and China
took three hundred and ninety-eight

22
00:01:11,671 --> 00:01:16,801
different tomato varieties and
analysed what is actually in them.

23
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:20,395
Then they sat panels of people
down to taste a selection of

24
00:01:20,475 --> 00:01:24,305
them, to find out which of those
chemicals we actually notice.

25
00:01:25,189 --> 00:01:26,979
The answer was not subtle.

26
00:01:27,769 --> 00:01:30,329
Modern commercial varieties
have significantly less

27
00:01:30,409 --> 00:01:33,439
of the compounds that make
a tomato taste of tomato.

28
00:01:36,659 --> 00:01:40,989
So let's back up. Why are
tomatoes and cucumbers everywhere?

29
00:01:41,699 --> 00:01:44,749
They are incredibly convenient.
You don't have to cook them.

30
00:01:44,939 --> 00:01:47,429
You don't even really
have to prepare them.

31
00:01:48,089 --> 00:01:50,209
Wash. Slice. Put on
plate. Congratulations.

32
00:01:50,289 --> 00:01:51,309
You have vegetables.

33
00:01:51,389 --> 00:01:57,199
They also work with almost everything.
Meat? Cucumber and tomato. Sandwich?

34
00:01:57,449 --> 00:01:58,819
Cucumber and tomato.

35
00:01:59,729 --> 00:02:03,889
Burger? Tomato. Salad? Tomato and
cucumber. Breakfast? You guessed it.

36
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:06,959
And if you're lucky...
someone brings a pepper.

37
00:02:07,289 --> 00:02:10,099
It's basically the default
vegetable starter pack.

38
00:02:10,905 --> 00:02:12,355
But there is one problem.

39
00:02:13,225 --> 00:02:15,665
The vegetables that are easiest
to eat aren't necessarily

40
00:02:15,745 --> 00:02:18,115
the vegetables with the
most interesting flavours.

41
00:02:19,537 --> 00:02:21,987
A tomato can be absolutely fantastic.

42
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:25,597
A really ripe one, grown
well, can be sweet,

43
00:02:25,677 --> 00:02:28,627
acidic, aromatic and
incredibly flavourful.

44
00:02:29,299 --> 00:02:32,589
But that is not necessarily
what we get in a supermarket.

45
00:02:33,768 --> 00:02:38,128
A commercial tomato has to survive
harvesting, packaging, transport,

46
00:02:38,208 --> 00:02:42,178
storage, and sitting on a shelf
without turning into tomato soup.

47
00:02:42,952 --> 00:02:46,662
So there are good reasons to choose
varieties that are firm and durable.

48
00:02:47,612 --> 00:02:50,162
And sometimes that comes
at the expense of flavour.

49
00:02:52,802 --> 00:02:54,732
Some of that is the price of durability.

50
00:02:55,242 --> 00:02:59,852
But one of the losses was not a
trade at all. It was an accident.

51
00:03:00,902 --> 00:03:03,552
Growers wanted tomatoes that ripen evenly.

52
00:03:04,302 --> 00:03:06,712
No dark green patch around the stem.

53
00:03:07,462 --> 00:03:09,592
The whole field ready on the same day.

54
00:03:10,303 --> 00:03:15,093
So breeders selected a variety that goes
uniformly pale green before it ripens.

55
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:18,233
And that trait is essentially one gene.

56
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:21,230
Here is the part nobody expected.

57
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:25,430
That gene builds the green
machinery inside the fruit itself.

58
00:03:26,371 --> 00:03:28,981
An unripe tomato is not just waiting.

59
00:03:29,831 --> 00:03:32,101
It is quietly photosynthesising.

60
00:03:32,471 --> 00:03:35,661
It makes part of its own sugar
while it hangs on the plant.

61
00:03:36,562 --> 00:03:39,062
Switch that gene off, and
you get exactly what was

62
00:03:39,142 --> 00:03:42,852
ordered: a tomato that
ripens beautifully evenly.

63
00:03:43,719 --> 00:03:47,209
You also get less sugar, and less
of the pigment that makes it red.

64
00:03:47,979 --> 00:03:49,509
Nobody chose that.

65
00:03:49,879 --> 00:03:53,529
They chose the way it looks, and
the sweetness quietly left with it.

66
00:03:56,529 --> 00:03:58,419
And then we finish the job at home.

67
00:03:59,329 --> 00:04:02,999
The aroma compounds in a tomato
are damaged below twelve degrees.

68
00:04:03,269 --> 00:04:07,219
And some of the genes that make
them never properly switch back on.

69
00:04:07,936 --> 00:04:09,726
So the fridge doesn't pause a tomato.

70
00:04:10,796 --> 00:04:13,466
It takes something away that
doesn't fully come back.

71
00:04:13,996 --> 00:04:17,266
And the cucumber has its own
version of the same story.

72
00:04:20,076 --> 00:04:23,906
A wild cucumber is bitter.
Aggressively bitter.

73
00:04:24,736 --> 00:04:28,786
It is full of a defence chemical that
discourages most things from eating it.

74
00:04:29,477 --> 00:04:32,027
Domestication switched
that off in the fruit.

75
00:04:32,857 --> 00:04:35,467
We bred the bitterness out
on purpose, and it worked.

76
00:04:36,295 --> 00:04:38,355
We asked agriculture
for vegetables that are

77
00:04:38,435 --> 00:04:41,085
firm, even, cheap, and
that don't fight back.

78
00:04:41,875 --> 00:04:43,345
We got exactly that.

79
00:04:46,285 --> 00:04:48,905
So if your entire experience
with vegetables is

80
00:04:48,985 --> 00:04:51,615
supermarket cucumber and
supermarket tomato...

81
00:04:52,705 --> 00:04:54,955
I can understand why you might conclude:

82
00:04:55,819 --> 00:05:00,049
"I don't like vegetables." Maybe
you don't dislike vegetables.

83
00:05:00,939 --> 00:05:02,819
Maybe you just dislike these two.

84
00:05:02,899 --> 00:05:04,549
Because here's the funny thing.

85
00:05:05,259 --> 00:05:06,949
Vegetables aren't one thing.

86
00:05:09,299 --> 00:05:13,589
There are dozens and dozens of completely
different plants we eat as vegetables.

87
00:05:14,579 --> 00:05:16,669
And they don't all taste remotely alike.

88
00:05:17,525 --> 00:05:19,475
Take carrots. You can eat them raw.

89
00:05:19,905 --> 00:05:24,115
But roast them with a little oil and they
turn sweeter and far more interesting.

90
00:05:24,545 --> 00:05:29,815
Or broccoli. You don't have to boil
it until it becomes a sad green cloud.

91
00:05:30,683 --> 00:05:34,473
Roast it. Grill it. Add
garlic. Add some spices.

92
00:05:35,223 --> 00:05:37,433
And it becomes an entirely different food.

93
00:05:38,271 --> 00:05:40,101
Cauliflower? Same story.

94
00:05:40,871 --> 00:05:44,201
Roasted cauliflower can have a
surprisingly rich, nutty flavour.

95
00:05:45,111 --> 00:05:48,461
And then there is cabbage.
Fresh. Cooked. Fermented.

96
00:05:49,591 --> 00:05:52,481
Suddenly we're not talking about
a boring side dish anymore.

97
00:05:54,781 --> 00:05:56,641
Personally, I think
vegetables are much more

98
00:05:56,721 --> 00:06:03,271
interesting when they actually have
an opinion. Radishes. Onions. Garlic.

99
00:06:03,401 --> 00:06:04,911
Horseradish. Chilli peppers.

100
00:06:05,827 --> 00:06:07,837
These don't sit quietly on your plate.

101
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:11,257
They arrive and announce:
"Hello. I have a flavour."

102
00:06:12,197 --> 00:06:15,447
And if you like spicy food,
chillies are almost cheating.

103
00:06:16,497 --> 00:06:19,947
A tiny amount can completely
change an otherwise boring meal.

104
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,490
And there are endless varieties.

105
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:23,800
Mild ones.

106
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:25,840
Smoky ones.

107
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:30,750
Ridiculously hot ones that exist mainly
to make you question your life choices.

108
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:35,790
This is another thing I find strange.

109
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:40,210
When people talk about eating
vegetables, they often imagine them raw.

110
00:06:41,197 --> 00:06:45,857
A few slices of cucumber. A
tomato. Maybe some lettuce.

111
00:06:45,937 --> 00:06:47,807
And that's supposed to be exciting.

112
00:06:48,757 --> 00:06:51,267
But cooking changes
vegetables dramatically.

113
00:06:52,392 --> 00:06:54,692
Roast a beetroot. Grill a pepper.

114
00:06:54,772 --> 00:06:56,342
Caramelise an onion.

115
00:06:56,452 --> 00:06:58,542
Bake a whole cauliflower.

116
00:06:59,297 --> 00:07:03,247
And suddenly vegetables aren't just
something sitting beside your actual food.

117
00:07:03,717 --> 00:07:05,047
They are the food.

118
00:07:07,687 --> 00:07:12,117
And this might actually explain why some
people say: "I don't like vegetables."

119
00:07:12,967 --> 00:07:16,677
Maybe they were simply introduced
to them in the worst possible way.

120
00:07:17,428 --> 00:07:19,828
Boiled broccoli. Overcooked cauliflower.

121
00:07:19,908 --> 00:07:21,738
Frozen spinach.

122
00:07:21,828 --> 00:07:25,118
A cucumber that has spent
three days in the fridge.

123
00:07:25,891 --> 00:07:28,941
And then someone says: "You
need to eat your vegetables."

124
00:07:29,951 --> 00:07:32,461
That's not exactly a
great marketing campaign.

125
00:07:33,391 --> 00:07:35,541
Imagine doing the same
thing with potatoes.

126
00:07:36,711 --> 00:07:39,361
Boil them without salt. Don't season them.

127
00:07:39,711 --> 00:07:41,461
Leave them until they're cold.

128
00:07:42,302 --> 00:07:45,332
And then tell a child:
"See? Potatoes are healthy."

129
00:07:46,062 --> 00:07:47,992
They might never eat a potato again.

130
00:07:50,522 --> 00:07:54,772
The good news is that you don't need to
force yourself to eat vegetables you hate.

131
00:07:55,525 --> 00:07:58,465
There isn't a law saying:
"Every human must consume

132
00:07:58,545 --> 00:08:02,055
exactly one cucumber and
half a tomato every evening."

133
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:07,200
You can choose. And the choice is much
wider than the three things on the plate.

134
00:08:08,070 --> 00:08:13,560
When did anyone last offer you
kohlrabi? Or celeriac? Or asparagus?

135
00:08:14,413 --> 00:08:15,613
These are not exotic.

136
00:08:16,533 --> 00:08:20,643
They are in the same shop, a few steps
away, and nobody ever mentions them.

137
00:08:21,295 --> 00:08:24,105
And any of them can be
eaten raw, roasted, grilled,

138
00:08:24,475 --> 00:08:27,825
fermented, pickled, or stuffed
into something else entirely.

139
00:08:28,665 --> 00:08:32,955
And just to be fair... I'm not saying
tomatoes and cucumbers are bad.

140
00:08:33,805 --> 00:08:35,815
A really good tomato? Fantastic.

141
00:08:36,305 --> 00:08:39,975
A fresh cucumber on a hot
summer day? Also fantastic.

142
00:08:41,083 --> 00:08:44,313
The problem is not that
tomatoes and cucumbers exist.

143
00:08:45,363 --> 00:08:47,003
The problem is that we have somehow turned

144
00:08:47,083 --> 00:08:49,373
them into the default
definition of vegetables.

145
00:08:50,419 --> 00:08:53,149
As if the vegetable
department had two products...

146
00:08:53,879 --> 00:08:56,369
and occasionally a pepper
gets through security.

147
00:08:59,129 --> 00:09:01,259
And there is actually
a bigger lesson here.

148
00:09:01,929 --> 00:09:04,399
If somebody tells you: "You
should eat more vegetables."

149
00:09:05,509 --> 00:09:08,699
The answer doesn't have to
be: "Oh no. More cucumber."

150
00:09:09,447 --> 00:09:12,117
It can be: "What vegetables
do I actually like?"

151
00:09:12,427 --> 00:09:14,177
Maybe it's roasted carrots.

152
00:09:14,887 --> 00:09:16,517
Maybe it's spicy peppers.

153
00:09:16,707 --> 00:09:18,667
Maybe it's sauerkraut.

154
00:09:18,747 --> 00:09:21,357
Maybe it's something
you've never even tried.

155
00:09:22,159 --> 00:09:24,189
Because vegetables aren't a punishment.

156
00:09:24,499 --> 00:09:27,589
And they certainly don't
have to taste like water.

157
00:09:28,423 --> 00:09:31,143
So next time someone puts a
plate in front of you containing

158
00:09:31,223 --> 00:09:34,873
tomato, cucumber, and, in
the better case, a pepper...

159
00:09:35,687 --> 00:09:38,577
remember: this isn't the
entire vegetable kingdom.

160
00:09:39,207 --> 00:09:40,697
It's barely the tutorial.

161
00:09:43,547 --> 00:09:47,617
And somewhere out there there is a
vegetable you might actually enjoy eating.

162
00:09:48,407 --> 00:09:49,997
You just have to find it.

163
00:09:50,663 --> 00:09:53,333
If you want the next one,
there is a subscribe button.
